WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.216
Lesson two point three.

2
00:00:01.525 --> 00:00:03.639
How a MIDI file's notes land on our grid

3
00:00:03.639 --> 00:00:04.361
of sixteenths.

4
00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:09.931
A MIDI file doesn't store seconds.

5
00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:12.344
It counts time in ticks: whole numbers

6
00:00:12.344 --> 00:00:13.116
that only go up.

7
00:00:13.406 --> 00:00:15.336
Its header says how many ticks make one

8
00:00:15.336 --> 00:00:15.577
beat.

9
00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:17.846
In the data primer's sample file, four

10
00:00:17.846 --> 00:00:18.763
hundred and eighty.

11
00:00:19.052 --> 00:00:20.838
So a sixteenth note is a hundred and

12
00:00:20.838 --> 00:00:22.865
twenty ticks, and a bar of four four is

13
00:00:22.865 --> 00:00:24.650
one thousand nine hundred and twenty.

14
00:00:25.079 --> 00:00:27.264
Each message is stored with its gap since

15
00:00:27.264 --> 00:00:28.772
the one before: a delta.

16
00:00:29.084 --> 00:00:31.425
Add the deltas up, and every note gets its

17
00:00:31.425 --> 00:00:33.921
tick: nought, four hundred and eighty,

18
00:00:33.921 --> 00:00:35.117
nine hundred and sixty.

19
00:00:36.899 --> 00:00:39.397
A tick gets a length from a tempo event:

20
00:00:39.397 --> 00:00:41.677
the number of microseconds one beat lasts.

21
00:00:42.003 --> 00:00:44.230
So divide a note's tick by the ticks per

22
00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:46.727
beat, to count beats, and multiply by the

23
00:00:46.727 --> 00:00:49.280
microseconds per beat over a million: the

24
00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:50.203
seconds per beat.

25
00:00:50.629 --> 00:00:52.567
The sample file's tempo event says four

26
00:00:52.567 --> 00:00:54.603
hundred and twenty-eight thousand, five

27
00:00:54.603 --> 00:00:56.784
hundred and seventy-one microseconds: a

28
00:00:56.784 --> 00:00:58.481
hundred and forty beats per minute.

29
00:00:58.772 --> 00:01:00.710
So tick five thousand seven hundred and

30
00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:02.891
sixty is twelve beats in, and twelve times

31
00:01:02.891 --> 00:01:04.733
zero point four two eight six is five

32
00:01:04.733 --> 00:01:05.848
point one four seconds.

33
00:01:06.139 --> 00:01:08.126
At this tempo, one tick is less than a

34
00:01:08.126 --> 00:01:08.708
millisecond.

35
00:01:12.167 --> 00:01:14.573
When a tempo event changes the tempo, the

36
00:01:14.573 --> 00:01:16.596
conversion changes from that tick on.

37
00:01:16.924 --> 00:01:19.221
The list of tempo events is the tempo map.

38
00:01:19.549 --> 00:01:22.065
Seconds are worked out piece by piece: a

39
00:01:22.065 --> 00:01:24.307
straight line between changes, steeper

40
00:01:24.307 --> 00:01:25.729
where the music is slower.

41
00:01:26.437 --> 00:01:28.563
The sample file plays at a hundred and

42
00:01:28.563 --> 00:01:30.852
forty, then drops to thirty-eight beats

43
00:01:30.852 --> 00:01:33.251
per minute for its last six seconds: a

44
00:01:33.251 --> 00:01:34.886
sequenced ending slowing down.

45
00:01:35.213 --> 00:01:37.448
Our store converts every note to seconds

46
00:01:37.448 --> 00:01:39.410
once, through each file's own map.

47
00:01:42.943 --> 00:01:45.186
Bars aren't stored in a MIDI file either.

48
00:01:45.514 --> 00:01:48.084
They're implied by time-signature events:

49
00:01:48.084 --> 00:01:50.545
four four, then two four, then four four

50
00:01:50.545 --> 00:01:50.873
again.

51
00:01:51.202 --> 00:01:53.389
Here's a song with one short bar of two

52
00:01:53.389 --> 00:01:53.663
four.

53
00:01:53.991 --> 00:01:56.233
A crash marks the first beat of each bar

54
00:01:56.233 --> 00:01:56.725
after it.

55
00:01:57.168 --> 00:01:59.565
Count bars of four four from tick zero,

56
00:01:59.565 --> 00:02:01.963
and every bar after the short one is half

57
00:02:01.963 --> 00:02:04.646
a bar out: each crash lands on beat three.

58
00:02:05.135 --> 00:02:07.284
So since the ninth of September, bars

59
00:02:07.284 --> 00:02:09.540
restart wherever the signature changes,

60
00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:10.882
each with its own length.

61
00:02:11.205 --> 00:02:12.977
The crashes are back on beat one.

62
00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:15.501
A window only ever covers two whole bars

63
00:02:15.501 --> 00:02:16.683
of four four in a row.

64
00:02:17.005 --> 00:02:19.422
So the short bar carries none, and neither

65
00:02:19.422 --> 00:02:21.463
does the lone four four bar before it.

66
00:02:23.723 --> 00:02:25.778
A lead-in is the same problem at the

67
00:02:25.778 --> 00:02:26.111
start.

68
00:02:26.445 --> 00:02:28.722
When a file's first time signature comes

69
00:02:28.722 --> 00:02:31.332
after tick zero, bars start there, and the

70
00:02:31.332 --> 00:02:33.220
part before it carries no windows.

71
00:02:33.878 --> 00:02:36.150
In Lakh, four thousand three hundred and

72
00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:38.474
fifty-six songs, eight percent, have at

73
00:02:38.474 --> 00:02:40.323
least one bar that isn't four four.

74
00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:42.753
And about three percent put their first

75
00:02:42.753 --> 00:02:44.391
time signature after tick zero.

76
00:02:47.877 --> 00:02:49.881
Fifty-two thousand songs can't be checked

77
00:02:49.881 --> 00:02:51.743
by ear, so the fix was tested with a

78
00:02:51.743 --> 00:02:52.029
probe.

79
00:02:52.315 --> 00:02:53.843
Crashes mark the start of a bar.

80
00:02:54.129 --> 00:02:56.086
So of the crash hits on beat one or beat

81
00:02:56.086 --> 00:02:57.756
three, what share is on beat one?

82
00:02:58.043 --> 00:03:00.095
c one counts the crashes on beat one:

83
00:03:00.095 --> 00:03:01.861
slots nought and sixteen of a window.

84
00:03:02.147 --> 00:03:04.390
c three counts those on beat three: slots

85
00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:05.440
eight and twenty-four.

86
00:03:05.893 --> 00:03:08.297
A song with eight crashes on beat one and

87
00:03:08.297 --> 00:03:11.044
two on beat three scores eight out of ten:

88
00:03:11.044 --> 00:03:11.902
eighty percent.

89
00:03:12.246 --> 00:03:14.707
Only songs with at least four such crashes

90
00:03:14.707 --> 00:03:15.050
count.

91
00:03:15.601 --> 00:03:17.816
On the three thousand seven hundred and

92
00:03:17.816 --> 00:03:19.755
fifteen affected songs with enough

93
00:03:19.755 --> 00:03:22.191
crashes, the share was fifty-nine percent

94
00:03:22.191 --> 00:03:24.406
with bars counted from tick zero, and

95
00:03:24.406 --> 00:03:26.788
eighty-three point seven percent after the

96
00:03:26.788 --> 00:03:27.009
fix.

97
00:03:27.451 --> 00:03:29.831
Song by song: two thousand three hundred

98
00:03:29.831 --> 00:03:32.159
and thirty-four improved, one thousand and

99
00:03:32.159 --> 00:03:34.435
ninety-one didn't change, and two hundred

100
00:03:34.435 --> 00:03:35.521
and ninety got worse.

101
00:03:37.032 --> 00:03:38.901
The first version of the rule restarted

102
00:03:38.901 --> 00:03:40.536
bars at every time-signature event.

103
00:03:40.816 --> 00:03:42.825
But files often restate the same signature

104
00:03:42.825 --> 00:03:43.432
at odd ticks.

105
00:03:43.712 --> 00:03:45.534
Here four four is restated at tick one

106
00:03:45.534 --> 00:03:47.216
thousand, part-way through a beat.

107
00:03:47.496 --> 00:03:49.832
Restart there, and the bar line moves: the

108
00:03:49.832 --> 00:03:51.701
crash at tick one thousand nine hundred

109
00:03:51.701 --> 00:03:53.569
and twenty is now seven point six seven

110
00:03:53.569 --> 00:03:54.223
sixteenths in.

111
00:03:54.504 --> 00:03:55.812
Slot eight: beat three.

112
00:03:56.232 --> 00:03:58.303
That first version made one thousand and

113
00:03:58.303 --> 00:04:00.274
ninety-six songs worse, on the first

114
00:04:00.274 --> 00:04:01.789
probe, which used the snare.

115
00:04:02.092 --> 00:04:04.113
After two fixes, restarting bars only

116
00:04:04.113 --> 00:04:06.286
where the signature changes, and probing

117
00:04:06.286 --> 00:04:08.357
with the crash, two hundred and ninety

118
00:04:08.357 --> 00:04:08.913
were worse.

119
00:04:09.216 --> 00:04:11.186
A rule that was right in principle was

120
00:04:11.186 --> 00:04:13.409
wrong on this data, until it was measured.

121
00:04:17.117 --> 00:04:19.248
Lesson one point two placed a note by

122
00:04:19.248 --> 00:04:21.042
rounding it to the nearest slot.

123
00:04:21.379 --> 00:04:23.734
Here's the whole of Equation five, with

124
00:04:23.734 --> 00:04:24.688
the part we keep.

125
00:04:25.024 --> 00:04:27.212
Delta sixteen is one sixteenth at this

126
00:04:27.212 --> 00:04:29.904
tempo: sixty over four times the beats per

127
00:04:29.904 --> 00:04:30.296
minute.

128
00:04:30.633 --> 00:04:32.988
At a hundred and twenty, an eighth of a

129
00:04:32.988 --> 00:04:33.381
second.

130
00:04:33.717 --> 00:04:35.961
x is how many sixteenths the onset sits

131
00:04:35.961 --> 00:04:38.148
after its bar line: a real number.

132
00:04:38.484 --> 00:04:40.615
s rounds it to the nearest whole slot.

133
00:04:40.952 --> 00:04:43.644
And O is what rounding threw away: x minus

134
00:04:43.644 --> 00:04:46.168
s, somewhere between minus a half and plus

135
00:04:46.168 --> 00:04:46.560
a half.

136
00:04:47.045 --> 00:04:49.396
Take a snare zero point three one seconds

137
00:04:49.396 --> 00:04:50.460
after the bar line.

138
00:04:50.796 --> 00:04:53.146
x is zero point three one over zero point

139
00:04:53.146 --> 00:04:55.385
one two five: two point four eight.

140
00:04:55.721 --> 00:04:58.016
That rounds to slot two, and O is plus

141
00:04:58.016 --> 00:05:00.591
zero point four eight: the hit is nearly

142
00:05:00.591 --> 00:05:02.830
half a slot late, and the store keeps

143
00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:03.109
that.

144
00:05:05.579 --> 00:05:07.752
In the code, the slot lines are laid out

145
00:05:07.752 --> 00:05:10.178
in ticks first: a quarter of the ticks per

146
00:05:10.178 --> 00:05:12.250
beat apart, from the start of a run of

147
00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:13.008
four four bars.

148
00:05:13.312 --> 00:05:15.232
Then each line is turned into seconds

149
00:05:15.232 --> 00:05:16.344
through the tempo map.

150
00:05:16.647 --> 00:05:18.972
So Delta sixteen is the local sixteenth:

151
00:05:18.972 --> 00:05:21.247
when the tempo changes, the slots after it

152
00:05:21.247 --> 00:05:22.055
stretch with it.

153
00:05:22.475 --> 00:05:24.791
Each onset's position is read between its

154
00:05:24.791 --> 00:05:26.886
two neighbouring lines: that's x.

155
00:05:27.217 --> 00:05:29.422
Floor of x plus a half rounds it to the

156
00:05:29.422 --> 00:05:30.139
nearest slot.

157
00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:32.731
And x minus the slot, the offset, is

158
00:05:32.731 --> 00:05:33.944
handed back beside it.

159
00:05:37.713 --> 00:05:39.950
A drummer is never exactly on the line.

160
00:05:40.294 --> 00:05:42.358
Push a snare meant for beat two ten

161
00:05:42.358 --> 00:05:44.881
milliseconds late: O becomes plus zero

162
00:05:44.881 --> 00:05:47.233
point zero eight, and it stays in slot

163
00:05:47.233 --> 00:05:47.519
four.

164
00:05:48.026 --> 00:05:50.118
Twenty milliseconds: plus zero point one

165
00:05:50.118 --> 00:05:50.300
six.

166
00:05:50.573 --> 00:05:52.302
Thirty: plus zero point two four.

167
00:05:52.575 --> 00:05:53.302
Still slot four.

168
00:05:53.575 --> 00:05:55.577
To change slot, a hit has to miss by more

169
00:05:55.577 --> 00:05:57.578
than half a sixteenth: sixty-two and a

170
00:05:57.578 --> 00:05:59.625
half milliseconds at a hundred and twenty,

171
00:05:59.625 --> 00:06:00.625
less at faster tempos.

172
00:06:00.898 --> 00:06:02.854
A typical human looseness of ten to thirty

173
00:06:02.854 --> 00:06:04.901
milliseconds, a common estimate we haven't

174
00:06:04.901 --> 00:06:06.493
measured, stays inside that edge.

175
00:06:06.925 --> 00:06:09.343
In Lakh, the average drum hit misses its

176
00:06:09.343 --> 00:06:11.592
line by just one point two percent of a

177
00:06:11.592 --> 00:06:12.154
sixteenth.

178
00:06:12.492 --> 00:06:14.797
For these files, rounding recovers the

179
00:06:14.797 --> 00:06:17.271
slot that was meant, and the offset keeps

180
00:06:17.271 --> 00:06:17.777
the rest.

181
00:06:19.125 --> 00:06:20.547
What does get there is swing.

182
00:06:20.842 --> 00:06:23.146
Straight eighths split each beat in half:

183
00:06:23.146 --> 00:06:24.029
slots two and six.

184
00:06:24.323 --> 00:06:26.285
Swung, the second eighth of each beat

185
00:06:26.285 --> 00:06:28.246
comes two thirds of the way through the

186
00:06:28.246 --> 00:06:28.491
beat.

187
00:06:29.173 --> 00:06:31.311
In sixteenths that's two point six seven.

188
00:06:31.624 --> 00:06:33.761
Past sixty-two and a half percent of the

189
00:06:33.761 --> 00:06:36.211
beat, rounding goes the other way: slot

190
00:06:36.211 --> 00:06:37.775
three, with O minus a third.

191
00:06:38.088 --> 00:06:40.382
And the same on every beat: slot seven.

192
00:06:40.695 --> 00:06:42.676
So the grid hears a swung eighth as a

193
00:06:42.676 --> 00:06:44.501
syncopated sixteenth, every time.

194
00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:47.484
About one song in ten: nine point nine

195
00:06:47.484 --> 00:06:49.769
percent of Lakh songs have their median

196
00:06:49.769 --> 00:06:52.282
drum onset on a triplet grid, where swing

197
00:06:52.282 --> 00:06:52.625
lives.

198
00:06:52.968 --> 00:06:55.253
For those songs the sixteenth grid is a

199
00:06:55.253 --> 00:06:56.852
limit of the representation.

200
00:06:57.195 --> 00:06:59.137
A swing-aware grid is future work.

201
00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:05.154
Each two-bar window becomes four arrays:

202
00:07:05.154 --> 00:07:05.925
Equation four.

203
00:07:06.256 --> 00:07:08.680
H is thirty-two slots by nine drums, with

204
00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:10.167
a one wherever a drum hits.

205
00:07:10.498 --> 00:07:13.142
Here, the year explainer's example: kick

206
00:07:13.142 --> 00:07:15.511
on beats one and three, snare on two and

207
00:07:15.511 --> 00:07:17.439
four, closed hat on every eighth.

208
00:07:17.936 --> 00:07:20.622
V has the same shape: each hit's velocity,

209
00:07:20.622 --> 00:07:22.342
over a hundred and twenty-seven.

210
00:07:22.664 --> 00:07:23.524
Paler is softer.

211
00:07:23.963 --> 00:07:26.602
O has it again: each hit's offset from its

212
00:07:26.602 --> 00:07:28.910
slot line, as a fraction of a sixteenth.

213
00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:31.494
The marks sit early or late inside their

214
00:07:31.494 --> 00:07:31.824
cells.

215
00:07:32.302 --> 00:07:34.800
And g is thirty-two numbers: the slots

216
00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:36.559
where the guitar's onsets fall.

217
00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:39.284
The store keeps the guitar's velocity per

218
00:07:39.284 --> 00:07:41.441
slot beside it, but not its offsets.

219
00:07:41.873 --> 00:07:44.285
Two hits of one drum in one slot keep the

220
00:07:44.285 --> 00:07:44.744
loudest.

221
00:07:45.089 --> 00:07:47.328
A drum note outside the nine voices is

222
00:07:47.328 --> 00:07:48.649
dropped, and counted.

223
00:07:49.177 --> 00:07:51.623
The drum model learns to predict H, from

224
00:07:51.623 --> 00:07:52.249
the guitar.

225
00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:54.980
V and O are kept for the battery, where

226
00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:57.256
feel is judged against real drummers in

227
00:07:57.256 --> 00:07:58.906
Groove, never against Lakh.

228
00:07:59.247 --> 00:08:01.694
Lesson two point eight turns these windows

229
00:08:01.694 --> 00:08:02.945
into training batches.

230
00:08:06.563 --> 00:08:08.070
Everything so far is shared.

231
00:08:08.392 --> 00:08:10.598
XMIDI's songs go through the same parser

232
00:08:10.598 --> 00:08:11.835
and the same grid code.

233
00:08:12.158 --> 00:08:13.556
What differs is the files.

234
00:08:13.879 --> 00:08:15.923
Every XMIDI file has four hundred and

235
00:08:15.923 --> 00:08:18.559
eighty ticks per beat, one tempo event: a

236
00:08:18.559 --> 00:08:20.765
hundred and nineteen beats per minute in

237
00:08:20.765 --> 00:08:22.863
ninety percent of songs, so the real

238
00:08:22.863 --> 00:08:23.723
tempos are lost.

239
00:08:24.046 --> 00:08:25.552
That's lesson two point six.

240
00:08:25.875 --> 00:08:28.403
And one time signature, four four, at tick

241
00:08:28.403 --> 00:08:28.672
zero.

242
00:08:28.995 --> 00:08:31.631
No lead-ins, no changes: the re-anchoring

243
00:08:31.631 --> 00:08:32.599
has nothing to do.

244
00:08:32.922 --> 00:08:35.504
Its drums also sit closer to the lines: in

245
00:08:35.504 --> 00:08:37.710
a sample of a thousand files, a median

246
00:08:37.710 --> 00:08:39.754
miss of zero point zero zero two of a

247
00:08:39.754 --> 00:08:41.959
sixteenth, against zero point zero one

248
00:08:41.959 --> 00:08:43.681
three for a thousand Lakh songs.

249
00:08:45.434 --> 00:08:47.871
One grid for both corpora: ticks become

250
00:08:47.871 --> 00:08:50.199
seconds through the tempo map, bars come

251
00:08:50.199 --> 00:08:52.527
from the time signatures, and each onset

252
00:08:52.527 --> 00:08:54.909
is rounded to the nearest sixteenth, with

253
00:08:54.909 --> 00:08:56.101
what's left over kept.

254
00:08:56.426 --> 00:08:59.025
Next: how Lakh is split by song, and the

255
00:08:59.025 --> 00:08:59.999
leak that remains.

